"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Dominant Strategies Analysis</h2>\n<p>✅ Analysis complete</p>\n<p>This analysis examines a <strong>Repeated Cournot Duopoly</strong>, where two firms (A and B) compete by choosing quantities ($q_A, q_B$) simultaneously in each period, with the market price determined by the total output ($Q = q_A + q_B$).</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 1: Game Structure Analysis</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Game Type</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-cooperative</strong>: Firms act in their own self-interest; any &quot;collusion&quot; must be self-enforcing (e.g., through trigger strategies).</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated</strong>: This is not a one-shot interaction. The game is played over multiple periods ($t = 1, 2, \\dots, T$ or $\\infty$). This allows for reputation building, rewards, and punishments.</li>\n<li><strong>Simultaneous (within periods)</strong>: In each round, firms choose quantities without knowing the other's choice for that specific round.</li>\n<li><strong>Continuous</strong>: Strategy spaces are typically continuous (firms can choose any non-negative quantity).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Strategy Spaces</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>One-shot Strategy</strong>: A single quantity choice $q_i \\in [0, \\infty)$.</li>\n<li><strong>Repeated Strategy</strong>: A complete plan of action that dictates the quantity to produce in period $t$ based on the entire history of play up to $t-1$. Examples include &quot;Always Cournot,&quot; &quot;Grim Trigger,&quot; or &quot;Tit-for-Tat.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Characterize Payoffs</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objective</strong>: Maximize the present value of the sum of profits over time: $V_i = \\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty} \\delta^t \\pi_{i,t}$, where $\\delta$ is the discount factor (patience).</li>\n<li><strong>Payoff Function</strong>: $\\pi_i(q_i, q_j) = q_i \\cdot [P(q_i + q_j) - c_i]$, where $P$ is the inverse demand function and $c_i$ is the marginal cost.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Key Features</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Information</strong>: Usually assumed <strong>Perfect Information</strong> regarding past moves (each firm sees what the other produced in previous rounds) but <strong>Imperfect Information</strong> regarding the current move.</li>\n<li><strong>Timing</strong>: Simultaneous moves in each stage.</li>\n<li><strong>Interdependence</strong>: Firm A’s profit is strictly decreasing in Firm B’s quantity (and vice versa) because higher total supply lowers the market price.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Part 2: Dominant Strategy Analysis</h3>\n<p>In a Cournot competition, the optimal choice for one firm depends entirely on the choice of the other. This leads to the following findings:</p>\n<h4>1. Strictly Dominant Strategies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>None.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Reasoning</strong>: There is no single quantity that is best regardless of the opponent's choice. If Firm B produces nothing ($q_B = 0$), Firm A’s best response is the monopoly quantity ($q_m$). If Firm B produces a very high quantity, Firm A’s best response is to produce a very small amount or exit the market. Because the &quot;best&quot; move changes based on the opponent's move, no strictly dominant strategy exists.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2. Weakly Dominant Strategies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>None.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Reasoning</strong>: For any quantity Firm A chooses, there is always a hypothetical quantity Firm B could choose that would make a different choice by Firm A more profitable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3. Dominated Strategies</h4>\n<p>While there are no dominant strategies, there are many <strong>dominated</strong> strategies (strategies that are always worse than another specific strategy):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Quantities exceeding the &quot;Best Response to Zero&quot;</strong>: Any quantity $q_i$ greater than the monopoly quantity $q_m$ is dominated.\n<ul>\n<li><em>Explanation</em>: If Firm B produces $q_B = 0$, $q_m$ is the profit-maximizing choice for Firm A. If Firm B produces $q_B &gt; 0$, the market price drops. In this case, producing $q_m$ is still better than producing anything <em>larger</em> than $q_m$, because the larger quantity would further depress the price and increase total costs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Quantities where Price &lt; Marginal Cost</strong>: Any quantity so high that it pushes the market price below the marginal cost $c$ regardless of the opponent's move is dominated by producing $q=0$.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4. Iteratively Eliminated Strategies</h4>\n<p>Through the process of <strong>Iterated Deletion of Strictly Dominated Strategies (IDSDS)</strong>, we can narrow the strategy space:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Step 1</strong>: Eliminate all $q_i &gt; q_m$ (as they are dominated by $q_m$).</li>\n<li><strong>Step 2</strong>: Knowing the opponent will never produce more than $q_m$, Firm A will never produce less than the Best Response to $q_m$.</li>\n<li><strong>Step 3</strong>: This iterative process continues, &quot;shrinking&quot; the range of rational quantities from both ends.</li>\n<li><strong>Convergence</strong>: In a standard linear Cournot model, this iterative elimination converges to a single point: the <strong>Cournot-Nash Equilibrium quantity ($q_{CN}$)</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Strategic Implications</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The &quot;Cournot Trap&quot;</strong>: In a one-shot game, the lack of a dominant strategy leads firms to the Nash Equilibrium, where they produce more and earn less than they would under a monopoly/collusive agreement.</li>\n<li><strong>The Power of Repetition</strong>: Because this is a <strong>repeated game</strong>, the &quot;Always Cournot&quot; strategy (playing the Nash quantity every round) is a subgame perfect equilibrium, but it is not the only one.</li>\n<li><strong>Incentive to Collude</strong>: Since $q_{collusive} &lt; q_{CN}$, firms have an incentive to restrict output to monopoly levels. However, because $q_{collusive}$ is not a dominant strategy, both firms face a &quot;Prisoner's Dilemma&quot; logic in every round: they want to cheat on the agreement to capture more market share.</li>\n<li><strong>Trigger Strategies</strong>: The analysis of dominated strategies explains why &quot;Grim Trigger&quot; works. By threatening to play the Cournot-Nash quantity (the result of iterative elimination) forever if the other firm cheats, firms can make cooperation ($q_{collusive}$) a rational choice, provided they value future profits enough (high $\\delta$).</li>\n<li><strong>Predictability</strong>: The fact that we can iteratively eliminate strategies toward the Nash Equilibrium suggests that in the absence of high trust or long-term horizons, the market will inevitably gravitate toward the Cournot-Nash output levels, resulting in lower prices for consumers but sub-optimal profits for the firms.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"